  "reply_to_user": "Hi Dana, thanks for letting us know. I checked your laptop (BL-LT-0101) and, as of its last check-in, its main drive has about 310 GB free out of 512 GB, which is roughly 60%. That's plenty, and we aren't seeing any warnings from it. So you don't need a cleanup right now. To work out what you're seeing, could you send a screenshot of the warning or copy its exact wording? It would also help to know roughly when it appeared and whether it mentions a specific drive, like a USB stick or an external drive. One more thing: if the message came up in a web browser, or asked you to call a number or download a 'cleaner' tool, please don't click it. Just send us the screenshot. If you'd like to free up space anyway, you can empty the Recycle Bin, clear out your Downloads folder, and go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files to remove temporary files.",
  "internal_note": "User is the CEO (VIP) and reports a Windows low-disk-space warning. P3. Checked device BL-LT-0101: online, last check-in 2026-10-09T14:16:57Z, 310 GB free of 512 GB (60.5%), no warnings. This is well above KB-017's 10% threshold, so the report doesn't match the telemetry. Possible causes: a warning about a secondary or removable drive, an old or stale warning, or a fake browser popup/scareware. I've asked the user for a screenshot, the exact wording, when it appeared, and which drive it mentions. I warned them not to act on popups asking them to call a number or install a cleaner, and shared the self-service cleanup steps from KB-017. No action needed for now. Re-check device status once the user replies. If it turns out to be scareware, look into possible PUP/malware.",
